     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Block Three Things, Then Defend",
     "content": "Keller prescribes blocking three things, in a deliberate order. First, block time off. Extraordinary performers schedule rest before work, because sustained high output requires recovery, and planning your time off first ensures you actually take it rather than letting work consume everything. Second, block your ONE Thing. Reserve a large chunk of your most productive hours, ideally four hours early each day, exclusively for your highest-priority work, and place it on the calendar as a recurring, non-negotiable appointment. Third, block planning time. Set aside a weekly slot to review your goals and connect your someday vision down to next week's ONE Thing, keeping the chain of priorities intact. Once the blocks are set, the real challenge is protection, and Keller offers four tactics drawn from the image of building a bunker. First, build a bunker: find or create a place where you can work undisturbed, away from interruption, whether a closed office, a library carrel, or a do-not-disturb signal. Second, store provisions: keep everything you need within reach, food, water, materials, so you never have to leave the bunker and break concentration. Third, sweep for mines: eliminate distractions before they detonate by silencing your phone, closing email, and disabling notifications that would pull you out. Fourth, enlist support: tell the people around you about your block so they respect it and help defend it rather than interrupt it. Keller stresses that protection is an active, ongoing effort, because the value of your block makes it a magnet for interruptions. The moment others learn you have open time, they will try to claim it. Your job is to treat the block as already spoken for, because it is, spoken for by your most important work. Blocking without protecting is a wish; blocking plus fierce protection is a system that reliably produces extraordinary results.",
